# Job: bootstrap the node framework's local dependency artifact, then run the node gate

You are a codex sub-agent under a Claude supervisor. Execution job. Read `AGENTS.md` (root) §2, §4.

## Situation
`framework/languages/node/package.json:58` pins `@zlink-systems/http-client` to
`file:../../../.artifacts/wsl/npm/zlink-systems-http-client-0.10.0.tgz`. That tarball is the pack
output of the framework's own workspace package `framework/languages/node/packages/http-client`
(`"files": ["dist","LICENSE"]`, `main: dist/index.js`) — it is NOT produced by
`scripts/local-package/build-wsl.sh` (that only packs the binding tarball, which exists:
`.artifacts/wsl/npm/zlink-systems-zlink-0.17.0.tgz`). The machine was reinstalled, so the tarball
is gone and `npm install` fails with ENOENT before anything else. `dist/` does not exist yet either,
and building it needs the root `node_modules` (typescript) — a bootstrap cycle.

Prior finding (previous machine, `doc/plan/c016-worklog/handoff-2026-09-04/fw-gate-node-summary.md`):
`npm ci` could not be used because the checked-in `package-lock.json` integrity for that tarball
differs from a locally packed one; `npm install --package-lock=false` was the working route.
Do not edit `package-lock.json` or `package.json`.

## What to do
1. Find how the repo itself expects this tarball to be produced — search `framework/languages/node/`
   scripts (`scripts/*.mjs`, `verify_packaged_contract.sh`, `README*`, `package.json` scripts) and
   `doc/`/`framework/doc/framework/node/guide/server/02-getting-started*.md` for the documented
   command. Prefer the documented route. If none exists, bootstrap minimally:
   (a) `npm pack ./packages/http-client --pack-destination ../../../.artifacts/wsl/npm` to get a
   placeholder tarball (empty dist is fine for install), (b) `TMPDIR=/dev/shm/zlink-tmp-node npm install
   --package-lock=false`, (c) `npm run build` (builds `packages/http-client/dist`), (d) re-pack the
   real tarball to the same path, (e) reinstall so `node_modules/@zlink-systems/http-client` has dist.
   Record the exact sequence in the summary so the supervisor can document it.
2. Run the node gate: `TMPDIR=/dev/shm/zlink-tmp-node flock -w7200 /tmp/zlink-node-gate.lock npm test`.
   If ESLint stops it at the known pre-existing `spot-timer.ts:137`, run the unit tests directly with
   the underlying runner from `package.json` and report both.
3. Run the node cross-language smoke: `framework/languages/node/cross-language/run_cross_language_smoke.sh`.
   If it needs the C++ host and that is not built, report and skip (do not build the C++ tree — another
   job owns `framework/languages/cpp/**`).
4. Then re-run the Java cross-language stage that was blocked only by the Node peer:
   `cd framework/languages/java && TMPDIR=/dev/shm/zlink-tmp-java flock -w7200 /tmp/zlink-jvm-gate.lock
   ./gradlew --no-daemon -p cross-language :Host:installDist` was already green; re-run
   `ZLINK_CPP_CROSS_LANGUAGE_STAGE=java-cross framework/languages/cpp/cross-language/run_cross_language_smoke.sh`
   (that script is a runner — running it is fine; do not modify anything under `framework/languages/cpp/`).

## Classify every failure: (A) DONTWAIT/backpressure, (B) terminal/error classification,
(C) known pre-existing (node lint `spot-timer.ts:137`; node sample SupportChat browser-harness),
(D) environment. Verify (C) by exact assertion/file:line, not counts.

